      "nets": ["unconnected-(U4-MS1{slash}AD0-Pad9)", "unconnected-(U4-MS2{slash}AD1-Pad10)", "unconnected-(U8-MS1{slash}AD0-Pad9)", "unconnected-(U8-MS2{slash}AD1-Pad10)", "unconnected-(U11-MS1{slash}AD0-Pad9)", "+3V3", "NET0057"],
      "observed": "The three TMC2209s share the UART net NET0057, but U4 and U8 have both address pins unconnected, and U11 has MS1/AD0 unconnected while MS2/AD1 is tied to +3V3. Thus U4 and U8 have identical address strapping/defaults on the shared bus.",
      "expected": "Drivers on a shared TMC2209 UART bus need distinct, defined address straps on MS1/AD0 and MS2/AD1 so each can be independently addressed.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"78\") (name \"NET0057\") ... (node (ref \"U11\") (pin \"14\") (pinfunction \"~{PD}/UART\") ...) (node (ref \"U4\") (pin \"14\") (pinfunction \"~{PD}/UART\") ...) (node (ref \"U8\") (pin \"14\") (pinfunction \"~{PD}/UART\") ...))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"140\") (name \"unconnected-(U4-MS1{slash}AD0-Pad9)\") ...)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"141\") (name \"unconnected-(U4-MS2{slash}AD1-Pad10)\") ...)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"152\") (name \"unconnected-(U11-MS1{slash}AD0-Pad9)\") ...)"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "a shared single-wire UART bus (`STEP_BUS`)"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "UART commands cannot reliably select U4 and U8 separately because they have the same address state; configuring or controlling drivers over the shared UART may affect both or produce conflicting responses. The firmware's current lack of UART use is separately documented, but does not resolve the hardware addressing issue.",
      "confidence": 0.91
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The shared TMC2209 UART bus has insufficient address strapping: at least two drivers are left with the same address state. This prevents reliable independent UART access to those drivers."
}

